[
    {
        "content": "<p>Iris-Lean is now based on Ordered Resource Algebras (ORAs)! This is a fairly involved change to the hierarchy of Iris-Lean, but it was designed to be backwards compatible with existing developments. It was developed by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1192816\">@Janine Lohse</span> , <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"917736\">@Sergei Stepanenko</span>, and myself.</p>\n<h2>What changed?</h2>\n<p>ORAs are like CMRAs, but also include a field for a custom ordering relation. The changes are live on <code>master</code>, and will be included in release 4.35 shortly.</p>\n<h2>What do I need to do?</h2>\n<p>If your Iris project just uses CMRAs from the library, there is almost nothing to change. The <code>CMRA</code> class still exists, and lemmas about the old <code>CMRA</code> order are still present under (mostly) the same names. A few namespaces have changed to be <code>ORA</code> rather than <code>CMRA</code>, and the data fields of the <code>CMRA</code> class have been split up into a small hierarchy (so some names are under <code>PCore</code>, <code>Op</code>, etc).</p>\n<p>If you define your own <code>CMRA</code>, you should use the <code>CMRA</code> smart constructor:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- For a given type X...</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">instance_reducible</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X.cmraData</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CMRAData</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- The usual CMRA stuff goes here, and you don't need to define an order</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CMRA</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ORA.ofCMRAData</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X.cmraData</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>We tested the new Iris on <code>HeapLang</code> and the <code>Clutch</code> projects; both were automatically adapted (staying with <code>CMRA</code>) without issue. </p>\n<h2>What's new?</h2>\n<p>Projects like Amaryllis or linear variants of Iris may want to use orders on their resources that are different from the usual extension order. With this change, the logics for these projects can be defined as a <code>UPred</code>, which now takes a unital <code>ORA</code> as an argument, rather than a unital <code>CMRA</code>.</p>\n<p>The backwards compatibility is based around a small family of typeclasses, specifying how an <code>ORA</code> order <code>≼ₒ</code> relates to the usual Iris extension order <code>≼</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IncOrd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Op</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Ordered</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">inc_ord</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≼</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≼ₒ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- (The indexed version of inc_ord is derived)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">OrdInc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Op</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Ordered</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">ord_inc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≼ₒ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≼</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">ordN_incN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≼ₒ</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≼</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsInc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Op</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Ordered</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IncOrd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">OrdInc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The library is fully generalized, adding <code>IsInc</code>, <code>IncOrd</code>, or <code>OrdInc</code> hypotheses where they are required. For example, while you can't lift local updates to regular updates in general, you can do so if your <code>ORA</code> is <code>OrdInc</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">auth_update</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OrdInc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">~</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"bp\">~&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a'</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b'</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"bp\">●</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Auth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">◯</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">~~&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">●</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">◯</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- ...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A <code>CMRA</code> is defined, literally, as an <code>ORA</code> whose order is equal to the extension order:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CMRA</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ORA</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsInc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Because <code>IsInc</code> is the strongest typeclass, a <code>CMRA</code> type will synthesize the new <code>IncOrd</code> or <code>OrdInc</code> requirements automatically. To reduce churn, lemmas stated using <code>≼</code> are still in the library, and are available for <code>IsInc</code> types. </p>\n<p>Our generic versions of the Iris <code>CMRA</code>s are mostly subtle adjustments of the ones found in William Mansky's Rocq <code>ORA</code> library. Our tweaks allow the resources to reduce to the usual Iris versions under <code>IncOrd</code> or <code>OrdInc</code> hypotheses. Two of Mansky's orders, for <code>Excl</code> and <code>DFrac</code>, are incompatible with the extension order, so they are not integrated into the library. Of course, these can still be implemented as custom <code>ORA</code>s, but the main branch of Iris-Lean currently only includes those generalizations that specialize to the usual <code>CMRA</code> ones.</p>\n<p>The initial goal was to let <code>ORA</code>-based developments use <code>UPred</code>, but we are able to allow some of them to use <code>IProp</code> as well. To enable this, in addition to being contractive, we require the resource's functor to be <code>IncOrd</code> (also called \"affine\"):</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">RFunctorAffine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">COFE.OFunctorPre</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">RFunctor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">affine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">COFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">COFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IncOrd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>All functors in base Iris are affine.</p>\n<p>Finally, we changed the definition of a view relation, adding a new field <code>op_left</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsViewRel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">UORA</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ViewRel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mono</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≡</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"bp\">≡</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≼ₒ</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">op_left</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rel_validN</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">✓</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rel_unit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">UORA.unit</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>We proved that this new definition is equivalent to the old one under the assumption <code>OrdInc</code>, and updated the view relations to work for general <code>ORA</code>s.</p>\n<h2>Help</h2>\n<p>If you have any questions about migrating to <code>ORA</code>, or about the changes we made, I'm happy to answer them!</p>",
        "id": 631668157,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1791987890
    }
]